* Transmission-gate DC probe (issue #106) -- ONE operating point, local.
* NOT a performance claim and NOT a record. This deck measures the on-state
* resistance of a COMPLEMENTARY PASS GATE -- both halves in parallel, as the
* cell builds them -- against the voltage it is passing, at one corner.
* Simulated (ngspice, sky130 device models); nothing here is measured.
*
* It exists because the `Sin_*` / `Sr_*` / `Mra_*` / `Mrb_*` gates in
* design/integrator/integrator.spice have no low-Vth half, so near vcm at a low
* supply neither half has overdrive: the NMOS half sees Vgs = Vdda - Vin and the
* PMOS half |Vgs| = Vin. The single-device `ron` kind in switch_probe.cir.tmpl
* cannot show that, because the quantity that matters is the PARALLEL
* conductance of the pair as the passed voltage moves across the input range.
*
* Van/Vap are 0 V ammeters, not circuit elements: each half still sits directly
* between the probe's drain node `d` and its source node `s`, so the operating
* point is identical to wiring both drains to `d` (checked: total current agrees
* to 7 digits). They make each half's share of the current readable, which is
* what says whether a remedy should widen the PMOS half or add a low-Vth NMOS.
*
* run_probe.py substitutes the doubled-brace placeholders: PDK library and
* corner, temperature, supply, the two device models and their geometries, the
* two gate biases, and the two terminal voltages.
.lib {{PDK_LIB}} {{CORNER}}
.temp {{TEMP}}
Vdda vdda 0 {{VDDA}}
* Source sits at the voltage being passed; drain is offset by the probe bias.
* The current through Vd is the whole gate current, so Ron = bias / I(Vd).
* NMOS half: bulk in the p-substrate at vssa. PMOS half: bulk in its n-well at
* vdda. Both are the static rails the committed netlist ties them to.
Vs s 0 {{VS}}
Vd d 0 {{VD}}
Vgn gn 0 {{VGN}}
Vgp gp 0 {{VGP}}
Van dn d 0
Vap dp d 0
X1 dn gn s 0 {{NMODEL}} {{NGEOM}}
X2 dp gp s vdda {{PMODEL}} {{PGEOM}}
.control
option gmin=1e-15
op
let i_dev = abs(i(vd))
let i_nmos = abs(i(van))
let i_pmos = abs(i(vap))
let r_on = {{BIAS}}/i_dev
print i_dev i_nmos i_pmos r_on
.endc
.end
